        "fitness" => """
            - Pulsepath is a workout and habit coach for busy people: a ten-minute session planned around the day, a few small habits around it (water, a walk after lunch, stretching before bed, lights out on time) and a streak with rest days built in.
            - Where it runs: the iPhone app is on the App Store now, for iOS 16 or later, and is the full app: every session, habits, reminders and steps read from Apple Health. The web app is live too, runs in any current browser and syncs with the phone through the same account. One account, one streak.
            - The web app can be installed on the device. In Safari on iPhone and iPad: Share, then Add to Home Screen. In Chrome on Android: the three-dot menu, then Install app or Add to Home screen. In Chrome or Edge on Windows, Linux and ChromeOS: the install icon at the end of the address bar, or Install in the browser menu. In Safari on a Mac: File, then Add to Dock. Without an install option, a bookmark works the same in a tab. The web app can't read steps from Apple Health, so the steps habit is iPhone only, and its reminders come as browser notifications.
            - Android: the app is in closed testing with 400 people and is planned for January 2027. The page has an Android waitlist: one email on release day, and if the date moves the waitlist hears first. Until then the web app works in Chrome on Android and can be installed on the home screen.
            - Coming later: Apple Watch, to follow the timer from the wrist, is being built after Android and has no date yet; a date comes once it's in testing. Training with a friend (a shared streak for two) is only being explored, maybe in 2027, and will only be built if testers keep asking.
            - Sessions take ten minutes and need no equipment: a chair and a wall are enough. A few sessions use a resistance band and say so before they start. Every session also has a five-minute version, and every move has an easier version. People can mark knees, back or shoulders to keep sessions gentle on them. The sample session on the page, Desk-break strength, has six moves: chair squats, wall push-ups, a forearm plank, reverse lunges, shoulder taps and a doorway chest stretch.
            - Streaks: everyone gets two rest days a week. Plan them ahead, or Pulsepath uses one when a day is missed. The streak only starts over after a third missed day in the same week.
            - Progress is counted in minutes, not calories, and Pulsepath asks nothing about weight unless the person wants it.
            - Prices: free for 3 sessions a week, with all the habit tracking. Plus adds every session, rest-day planning and the weekly review, for 4.99 euros a month or 39.99 euros a year. The first 14 days of Plus are free, and cancelling before day 14 costs nothing. Outside the euro area the App Store sets the price in the local currency.
            - Version 2.4, released on 21 October 2026: rest days (plan up to two a week, or let Pulsepath use one on a missed day), a five-minute version of every session, a soft tick in the last three seconds of each move, and a fix so a phone call no longer stops the timer for good. Version 2.3, released on 30 September 2026: habit stacking (tying a habit to the session, like a glass of water right after it), a weekly review on the last evening of the week, text that stays readable at the largest accessibility sizes, and a fix for streaks losing a day across time zones. Updates ship every three to four weeks.
            - Data: sessions and habits are stored in the EU, in Frankfurt, and are never sold or shared with advertisers. There are no ads, now or later; Plus pays for everything. Steps from Apple Health stay on the iPhone and only the daily total shows on the Today screen. Everything can be exported or deleted from Settings.
            - Pulsepath isn't medical advice. Anyone recovering from an injury should check with a doctor or physiotherapist first.
            - The people quoted on the page are Mateus Carvalho, father of two in Braga, Adaeze Nwosu, an ICU nurse in Manchester, and Hana Kobayashi, a translator in Osaka. The rating shown on the page comes straight from the App Store listing.
            - The company: Pulsepath ApS, Vesterbrogade 41, 1620 Copenhagen V, Denmark. Started in 2023 by Freja Holm (co-founder and CEO), a physiotherapist for nine years in Nørrebro, and Kofi Mensah (co-founder and CTO), who built banking apps in Accra and Copenhagen. Inés Navarro, a behaviour scientist, joined a year later as head of coaching and designed rest days after studying 3,000 broken streaks. 14 people, plus 3 physiotherapists who review every session before it ships.
            - History: the iPhone app launched in March 2025 with 40 sessions, the web app in June 2026, and Android closed testing started in October 2026. iPhone came first because the small team would rather ship one app done well than two done halfway. 2.4 million sessions have been finished since March 2025, and 61% of people who start are still training eight weeks later.
            - Contact: hello@pulsepath.example or +45 32 55 01 87 for questions and feedback, press@pulsepath.example for press. A person answers every email within two working days, usually sooner.
            """,
        "retail" => """
